<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Palpa</h1><p class="st-meta">1493 CE – 1806 CE (approx.) · capital Tansen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sen hill kingdom of western Nepal ruled from Tansen, once the most powerful of the Chaubise Rajya before falling to Gorkha.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Sen dynasty, claiming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> descent, established itself in the hills of Palpa in the late 15th century; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mukunda-sen">Mukunda Sen</a> is the best-known early ruler. He is said to have campaigned as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a>, and his realm, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tansen-nepal">Tansen</a>, was later divided among his sons into Palpa, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-makwanpur">Kingdom of Makwanpur</a> and other states.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Palpa was the leading state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaubise-rajya">Chaubise Rajya</a>, the twenty-four hill principalities of central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a>, and controlled trade routes from the Gangetic plain into the hills. Its kings intermarried with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-tanahun">Tanahun</a> royal families, and the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tansen-nepal">Tansen</a> developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newar-people">Newar</a> trading communities and temples.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Palpa resisted the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gorkha-kingdom">Gorkha</a> unification for decades, relying on marriage alliances with the Gorkha court and on its hill fortresses. It was finally annexed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal</a> in 1806, and its last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prithvipal-sen">Prithvipal Sen</a>, was killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1493 CE – 1806 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.41%;width:6.23%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1493 CE – 1806 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tansen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mukunda Sen, Prithvipal Sen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nepali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Nepal</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Palpa, Sen kingdom of Palpa</dd></dl></aside></div>

